# Supabase Rules

## Choose The Right Client

- In Client Components, use `getSupabase()` from `@lib/supabase/client.ts`.
- In Server Components and authenticated API routes, use `createSessionClient()` from `@lib/supabase/server.ts`.
- Use `createServiceClient()` only in trusted server-side code that intentionally bypasses RLS.
- Never expose the service-role client or key to the browser.

## Typing

- Type Supabase work with `Database` from `@/lib/supabase/types`.
- Reuse generated table and row types instead of inventing parallel shapes when the database shape is the source of truth.
- When schema changes are made, update generated types with `pnpm run db:types`.

## Querying

- Prefer explicit column lists in `.select()` calls over `select("*")` when practical.
- Check Supabase `error` results before assuming `data` is valid.
- Keep user-scoped reads and writes behind RLS-aware clients.
- Keep admin-only or system-level operations clearly separated when using the service client.

## Auth

- Use `supabase.auth.getUser()` for authenticated user checks in server code.
- Verify authorization server-side, not only in the UI.

## Project-Specific Guidance

- Browser access is already wrapped in a singleton helper.
- Server auth and service-role helpers already exist; reuse them rather than re-creating Supabase bootstrapping in each file.
- Keep database column names database-shaped in Supabase queries, then map to frontend-friendly shapes when needed.

## Prefer

- `const supabase = await createSessionClient()`
- `const supabase = getSupabase()`
- `const { data, error } = await supabase.from("profiles").select("name, surname").single()`

## Avoid

- Calling `createBrowserClient` directly in feature files
- Using `createServiceClient()` for routine user-scoped requests
- Ignoring returned Supabase errors

## Reference Files

- `@lib/supabase/client.ts`
- `@lib/supabase/server.ts`
- `@lib/supabase/types.ts`
